From: DroidDeck-Mali, after emir34-34/DroidDeck 8f18a5ad
Date: 2026-10-14
Subject: CSF: call the provoking-vertex helper with the full 64-bit address

Only the CSF backend has this path, so it is wrong in every arch that submits
through CSF (this tree: 10, 11, 12, 13, 14 - and 11 is the arch 0003 ports in
for Mali-G615/G715). On Android's kbase the addresses are SAME_VA and sit above
4 GB, so `fns_bo->addr.dev` does not fit in the uint32_t that held it: the
command stream frontend prefetched from the truncated address and every group
stopped (dmesg `TRANSLATION_FAULT ... source Command Stream Frontend, pref0`,
client log `CSF group N fatal error ... exception 0xc1`), which shows up as
queue timeouts, DEVICE LOST and a client whose memory grows without bound.

`get_fb_descs()` only takes this path when a render pass starts before the
provoking-vertex mode is known. Chromium on Zink does that on every pass and
vkcube does not, which is why the Steam client (CEF) is what provokes it.

Reported and fixed by emirvapur11 in emir34-34/DroidDeck, commit 8f18a5ad
("panvk: fix the real cause of Steam's UI freezing on Mali"), patch 0002, with
the fault and the fix measured on a Mali-G720 (MT6899) over ~5 minutes of Big
Picture input. Verified here against the fork's own source: the same line is in
wonderkast02/panvk-g720-kbase-csf g720-development ca16389 (line 2449).

--- a/src/panfrost/vulkan/csf/panvk_vX_cmd_draw.c
+++ b/src/panfrost/vulkan/csf/panvk_vX_cmd_draw.c
@@ -1849,7 +1849,10 @@ get_fb_descs(struct panvk_cmd_buffer *cmdbuf)
          uint32_t fn_idx = calc_fn_set_fbds_provoking_vertex_idx(cmdbuf);
          uint32_t fn_stride =
             dev->draw_ctx->fn_set_fbds_provoking_vertex_stride;
-         uint32_t fn_addr =
+         /* 64-bit: kbase's SAME_VA addresses sit above 4 GB, so holding this
+          * one in a uint32_t truncated it and the command stream frontend
+          * prefetched from the wrong address. */
+         uint64_t fn_addr =
             dev->draw_ctx->fns_bo->addr.dev + fn_idx * fn_stride;
          cs_move64_to(b, addr_reg, fn_addr);
          cs_move32_to(b, length_reg, fn_stride);
